<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>


<worksheet xml:id="ws-rings-activity">

	<title>Rings, Units, Zero Divisors, and Integral Domains</title>
	<introduction><p><alert>Almost Perfect</alert></p></introduction>
	
	<page>

	<paragraphs>
		<title>Definitions</title>
		
		<definition>
			<p>A <term>ring</term> is a set <m>R</m> together with two binary operations, called addition (<m>+</m>) and multiplication (<m>\cdot</m>), such that all of the following axioms hold:</p>
			
			<ul>
				<li><p><em>The set <m>R</m> is closed under addition and multiplication,</em> meaning that for all <m>x, y \in R</m>, <m>x+y \in R</m> and <m>x \cdot y \in R</m>.</p></li>
				
				<li><p><em>Addition is associative,</em> meaning that for all <m>x, y, z \in R</m>, <m>(x+y)+z = x+(y+z)</m>.</p></li>
				
				<li><p><em>Addition is commutative,</em> meaning that for all <m>x, y \in R</m>, <m>x +y = y+x</m>.</p></li>
				
				<li><p><em>The set <m>R</m> contains an additive identity,</em> also called a <em>zero element</em>, meaning that there exists some element <m>0_{R} \in R</m> such that <m>x+0_{R} = x</m> for all <m>x \in R</m>.</p></li>
				
				<li><p><em>Every element of <m>R</m> has an additive inverse within <m>R</m></em>, meaning that for every <m>x \in R</m>, there exists <m>y \in R</m> such that <m>x + y = 0_{R}</m>.</p></li>
				
				<li><p><em>Multiplication is associative,</em> meaning that for all <m>x, y, z \in R</m>, <m>(x \cdot y) \cdot z = x \cdot (y \cdot z)</m>.</p></li>
				
				<li><p><em>Multiplication distributes over addition,</em> meaning that for all <m>x, y, z \in R</m>, <m>x \cdot (y+z) = x \cdot y + x \cdot z</m> and <m>(x+y) \cdot z = x \cdot z + y \cdot z</m>.</p></li>
			</ul>
			
		</definition>
		<!-- <image source="images/Rings-Axioms.png" /> -->
    </paragraphs>
    </page>

	<page>
	<paragraphs>

<p>
  For each of the definitions below, list an example and a non-example.
  I.e., list something that is a commutative ring and something that is not a
  commutative ring.
  Some examples of number systems you might use: <m>\mathbb{N}</m>,
  <m>\mathbb{Z}</m>, <m>\mathbb{Z}_{5}</m>, <m>\mathbb{Z}_{6}</m>,
  <m>\mathbb{R}</m>, <m>\mathcal{M}_{2\times 2}(\mathbb{R})</m>,
  <m>\mathbb{E}</m> (the even numbers).
</p>

<exercise>
<p>
 <em>Commutative ring:</em> Let <m>R</m> be a ring.
  Then <m>R</m> is said to be <em>commutative</em> if multiplication in <m>R</m>
  is commutative, that is, if <m>xy=yx</m> for all <m>x,y\in R</m>.
</p>
        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
        	<p>Example:</p>
			<p>Nonexample:</p>
        </sidebyside>

</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>
 <em>Ring with identity:</em> Let <m>R</m> be a ring.
  An <em>identity</em> for <m>R</m> is an element <m>1_{R}\in R</m> such that
  <m>1_{R} \neq 0_{R}</m> and <m>1_{R} \cdot x = x = x\cdot 1_{R}</m> for all
  <m>x\in R</m>.
  If such an element exists, then <m>R</m> is said to be a
  <em>ring with identity</em>.
</p>
        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
        	<p>Example:</p>
			<p>Nonexample:</p>
        </sidebyside>

</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>
 <em>Zero divisors:</em> Let <m>R</m> be a ring.
  An element <m>x\in R</m> is said to be a <em>zero divisor</em> if
  <m>x\neq 0</m> and <m>xy=0</m> or <m>yx = 0</m> for some nonzero
  <m>y\in R</m>.
</p>
        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
        	<p>Example (ring and element):</p>
			<p>Nonexample:</p>
        </sidebyside>

</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>
  <em>Multiplicative inverse:</em> Let <m>R</m> be a ring with identity
  and let <m>x\in R</m>.
  An element <m>y\in R</m> is said to be a <em>multiplicative inverse</em> of
  <m>x</m> provided that <m>xy=1=yx</m>.
</p>
        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
        	<p>Example (ring and element):</p>
			<p>Nonexample:</p>
        </sidebyside>

</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>
 <em>Units:</em> Let <m>R</m> be a ring with identity.
  An element <m>x\in R</m> is said to be a <em>unit</em> provided that <m>R</m>
  contains a multiplicative inverse for <m>x</m>.
  In other words, <m>x\in R</m> is a unit if and only if there exists
  <m>y\in R</m> such that <m>xy=1=yx</m>.
</p>
        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
        	<p>Example (ring and element):</p>
			<p>Nonexample:</p>
        </sidebyside>

</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>
  <em>Integral Domain:</em> An <em>integral domain</em> is a commutative
  ring with identity that contains no zero divisors.
</p>
        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
        	<p>Example:</p>
			<p>Nonexample:</p>
        </sidebyside>

</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>
 <em>Field:</em> A <em>field</em> is a commutative ring with identity in
  which every nonzero element has a multiplicative inverse.
</p>
        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
        	<p>Example:</p>
			<p>Nonexample:</p>
        </sidebyside>

</exercise>

    </paragraphs>
	</page>
	
	
	<page>
	<paragraphs>
		<title>Units and Zero Divisors in <m>\mathbb{Z}_{n}</m></title>

  <p>
    In this activity we'll work to determine (and prove) exactly when an element of <m>\mathbb{Z}_{n}</m> is a unit or a zero divisor.
  </p>

	<exercise workspace="0.5in">
        <p>
          Look back at multiplication tables you have for various
          <m>\mathbb{Z}_{n}</m>.
          For at least four of them, list the units and the zero divisors.
        </p>

 		<sidebyside widths="5% 30% 30%">
			<p><m>n</m></p>
			<p>units</p>
			<p>zero divisors</p>
		</sidebyside>
  
          </exercise>
          
      <exercise>
      	<introduction>
        <p>
          Determining whether an element of <m>\mathbb{Z}_{n}</m> is a unit or a
          zero divisor has something to do with last week's module.
          What do you notice about the units and zero divisors in your lists?
          (Hint: Consider their relationship to <m>n</m>.) Then fill in the
          conjectures:
        </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
              <p>
                Let <m>n \in \mathbb{N}</m> with <m>n\ge 2</m>, and let
                <m>[a]\in \mathbb{Z}_{n}</m>.
                Then <m>[a]</m> is a unit in <m>\mathbb{Z}_{n}</m> if and only
                if...
              </p>
        </task>

        <task>
              <p>
                Let <m>n \in \mathbb{N}</m> with <m>n\ge 2</m>, and let
                <m>[a]\in \mathbb{Z}_{n}</m>.
                Then <m>[a] \neq [0]</m> is a zero divisor in
                <m>\mathbb{Z}_{n}</m> if and only if...
              </p>
       </task>

      </exercise>

      <exercise workspace="0.25in">
        <p>
          In last week's module you found <m>\gcd(27182,3141)=1</m> and
          <m>27182(-445)+3141(3851)=1</m>.
          Reduce both sides modulo <m>27182</m> to find the multiplicative
          inverse of <m>3141</m> in <m>Z_{27182}</m>.
        </p>
	 </exercise>

     <exercise>
     	<introduction>
        <p>
          Proofs!
          Next we'll prove the conjectures you made above. First, we'll prove the forward direction of the unit part.
         </p>
         
         <p>Suppose
                <m>n\in\mathbb{N}</m> with <m>n\ge 2</m> and
                <m>[a]\in \mathbb{Z}_{n}</m>.
                Suppose <m>[a]</m> is a unit in <m>\mathbb{Z}_{n}</m>.
         </p>
       	</introduction>
	
		<task workspace="0.5cm">
                    <p>
                      Write down what it means for <m>[a]</m> to be a unit in
                      <m>\mathbb{Z}_{n}</m>.
                    </p>
		</task>
		
        <task workspace="0.5cm">
                    <p>
                      Recall from Module 3 that two equivalence classes are
                      equal, <m>[x] = [y]</m>, if and only if <m>x\sim y</m>.
                      Translate your statement above using this fact.
                    </p>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.5cm">
                    <p>
                      Find a linear combination of <m>a</m> and <m>n</m> that is
                      equal to <m>1</m>.
                      What does this say about <m>\gcd(a,n)</m>?
                      (Hint: Bezout's identity.)
                    </p>
        </task>
		</exercise>
	</paragraphs>
</page>

<page>
<paragraphs>
		<exercise workspace="1.5in">
				<p>
                (Backward direction of the unit part.) Suppose
                <m>n\in \mathbb{N}</m> with <m>n\ge 2</m> and
                <m>[a]\in\mathbb{Z}_{n}</m>.
                Suppose <m>\gcd(a,n)=1</m>.
                Apply Bezout's identity and reduce modulo <m>n</m>.
                How does this show <m>[a]</m> is a unit in
                <m>\mathbb{Z}_{n}</m>?
              	</p>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise>
			<introduction>
				<p>
                (Forward direction of the zero divisor part.) Suppose
                <m>n\in \mathbb{N}</m> with <m>n\ge 2</m>,
                <m>[a]\in\mathbb{Z}_{n}</m> and <m>[a]\neq [0]</m>.
                Suppose <m>[a]</m> is a zero divisor in <m>\mathbb{Z}_{n}</m>.
                </p>
            </introduction>
            
            <task workspace="1.5in">
            	<p>
                     Write down a careful definition of what it means for
                      <m>[a]</m> to be a zero divisor.
                    </p>
             </task>
             
             <task workspace="1.5in">
             		<p>
                      Suppose <m>\gcd(a,n)=1</m>.
                      Apply Bezout's identity, and multiply the result by
                      <m>b</m>.
                      Conclude <m>n\mid b</m>.
                      Why is this a contradiction?
                    </p>
              </task>
          </exercise>
             
          <exercise workspace="1.5in">
          	 <p>
                (Finally: Backward direction of the zero divisor part.) Suppose
                <m>n\in \mathbb{N}</m> with <m>n\ge 2</m> and
                <m>[a]\in\mathbb{Z}_{n}</m> with <m>[a]\neq [0]</m>.
                Suppose <m>\gcd(a,n)&gt;1</m>.
                Let <m>\gcd(a,n)=d</m>.
                Show that <m>\frac{n}{d}</m> is an integer,
                <m>[ \frac{n}{d}] \neq [0]</m> and
                <m>[a] \cdot [\frac{n}{d}] = [0]</m>.
              </p>
          </exercise>
    </paragraphs>
	</page>

	<page>
		<exercise workspace="1.5in">
			<p>
          Be careful - not everything has to be a unit or a zero divisor (or
          <m>0_{R}</m> ).
          What are the units in <m>\mathbb{Z}</m>?
          What are the zero divisors in <m>\mathbb{Z}</m>?
          Are there elements that are neither units nor zero divisors?
        </p>
		</exercise>
		
		<paragraphs>
			<title>Polynomials</title>
	
			<definition>
				<p>Let <m>R</m> be a commutative ring.
    A <term>polynomial in <m>x</m> over <m>R</m> </term> is an expression of the form
    <md>
      a_{n}x^{n} + a_{n-1}x^{n-1}+ \cdots a_{2}x^{2} + a_{1}x^{1} + a_{0}x^{0},
    </md>
    where <m>n</m> is a nonnegative integer and
    <m>a_{n},a_{n-1},\dots,a_{2},a_{1},a_{0}</m> are elements of <m>R</m>.
    The <em>polynomial ring over <m>R</m> </em> is the set of all polynomials
    over the ring <m>R</m>, and is denoted <m>R[x]</m>.
    			</p>
			</definition>

	<exercise workspace="2in">
        <p>
          Is <m>\mathbb{Z}[x]</m> a commutative ring with identity?
          Is <m>\mathbb{E}[x]</m> a commutative ring with identity?
          Is <m>R[x]</m> always, sometimes or never a commutative ring with
          identity?
        </p>
     </exercise>

     <exercise workspace="2in">
        <p>
          Let <m>f(x)=[3]x+[4]</m> and <m>g(x) = [4]x+[3]</m> be polynomials in
          <m>\mathbb{Z}_{6}[x]</m>.
          Find <m>f(x)g(x)</m>.
          Is <m>R[x]</m> always, sometimes, or never an integral domain?
        </p>
	</exercise>
	
		</paragraphs>
	</page>
	

<page>
<paragraphs>
<title>Review questions</title>
<p>Make sure you can answer the following questions:</p>

<ol>
	<li><p>What's a ring? A commutative ring? A ring with identity?  A multiplicative inverse? A zero divisor? A unit? An integral domain? A field? Make sure you can give examples and nonexamples of each and varying combinations.</p></li>

	<li><p>How can you tell if $[a]$ in $\mathbb{Z}_n$ is a zero divisor or a unit? How can you use an algorithm to find the multiplicative inverse of $[a]$ in $\mathbb{Z}_n$?  Is everything in a ring a zero divisor, a unit, or zero?</p></li>

	<li><p>What's a polynomial ring? Are polynomial rings always commutative? Do they always have identity? Are they always integral domains?
</p></li>
</ol>
                
<p>The rest of this page is for you to summarize important ideas from the module.</p>

</paragraphs>

</page>


<page>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Weekly Practice 5</title>

		<exercise number="1">
		
		<introduction><p>
          Let <m>[a]\in\mathbb{Z}_{n}</m> with <m>n\in \mathbb{N}</m>,
          <m>n\ge 2</m>.
          Determine whether each of the following statements are true or false.
          Justify your answers.
          Not all of these are true!</p>
          </introduction>
          
          <task>
              <p>
                If <m>[a]</m> is a zero divisor, then <m>[a]</m> is not a unit.
              </p>
            </task>

            <task>
              <p>
                If <m>[a]</m> is not a unit, then <m>[a]</m> is a zero divisor.
              </p>
            </task>

            <task>
              <p>
                If <m>[a]</m> is a unit then <m>[a]</m> is not a zero divisor.
              </p>
            </task>

            <task>
              <p>
                If <m>[a]</m> is not a zero divisor, then <m>[a]</m> is a unit.
              </p>
            </task>
 		</exercise>
	
		<exercise number="2">
		<introduction><p>Back to units and zero divisors in <m>\mathbb{Z}_n</m>.</p></introduction>
        	<task><p>
                Consider <m>\mathbb{Z}_{24}</m>.
                Since 16 and 24 are not relatively prime, it should follow that
                <m>[16]_{24}</m> is a zero divisor in <m>\mathbb{Z}_{24}</m>.
                Let's show <m>[16]_{24}\in \mathbb{Z}_{24}</m> is a zero
                divisor.
                Find <m>d=\gcd(16,24)</m>.
                Show <m>b=\frac{24}{d}</m> is a nonzero integer, and
                <m>[16]_{24}[b]_{24}= [0]_{24}</m>.
              </p></task>
            <task><p>Generalize what you did in part (a) to <m>\mathbb{Z}_{n}</m>,
                thus showing that if <m>\gcd(a,n)&gt;1</m> then <m>[a]_{n}</m>
                is a zero divisor in <m>\mathbb{Z}_{n}</m>.</p></task>
                
            <task><p>Now, assume <m>[a]_{n}\in\mathbb{Z}_{n}</m> is a unit.
                Let's show <m>\gcd(a,n)=1</m>.
                Since <m>[a]_{n}</m> is a unit, there exists
                <m>[b]_{n}\in \mathbb{Z}_{n}</m> such that
                <m>[a]_{n}[b]_{n} = [1]_{n}</m>.
                This can be rewritten as <m>[ab]_{n} = [1]_{n}</m>.
                This means <m>[ab]_{n}</m> and <m>[1]_{n}</m> are equal as
                congruence classes.</p>
                
                <p>
                First, recall this Lemma from Module 3:
                <xref ref="lem-equivclasses" />
                </p>
                <!--
                <lemma>
                  <statement>
                    <p>
                      Let <m>S</m> be a nonempty set and let <m>\sim</m> be an
                      equivalence relation on <m>S</m>.
                      Then for all <m>a,b\in S</m>, <m>[a]=[b]</m> if and only
                      if <m>a\sim b</m>.
                    </p>
                  </statement>
                </lemma>
                -->
                
                <p>Apply the Lemma (here <m>\sim</m> is congruence modulo <m>n</m>
                ), and then work with what you get to show that there's a linear
                combination of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> equal to <m>1</m>.
                Then conclude that <m>\gcd(a,b)=1</m>.
			</p></task>
		</exercise>
		
